Title: Concrete Strength Prediction — Linear Regression and Transform Analysis

Overview:
This project focuses on predicting concrete compressive strength (in MPa) using classical linear regression models. The main goal is to understand which preprocessing techniques (such as standardization and log(x+1) transformation) improve model accuracy and generalization.


Dataset Description:
The dataset is from the UCI Machine Learning Repository and includes eight input features that affect concrete strength:

Cement (kg/m³)
Blast Furnace Slag (kg/m³)
Fly Ash (kg/m³)
Water (kg/m³)
Superplasticizer (kg/m³)
Coarse Aggregate (kg/m³)
Fine Aggregate (kg/m³)
Age (days)
Target variable: Concrete Compressive Strength (MPa)

Methodology:

Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA):
Studied correlation between predictors and target variable
Checked for skewness and outliers

Modeling Approaches:

Linear Regression with raw data
Linear Regression with standardized data
Linear Regression with log(x+1) transformed data

Evaluation Metrics:

Mean Squared Error (MSE)
R² Score

Model Performance Results:

MSE (Train vs Test):
The log(x+1) model cuts error almost in half compared to raw or standardized data.
Train MSE ≈ 55.8
Test MSE ≈ 54.4
This shows the log transformation reduces skewness and heteroscedasticity, giving a more accurate fit.

R² (Train vs Test):
Generalization improves significantly from about 0.37 (raw/standardized) to about 0.76 (log(x+1)), showing that the log-transform provides a better model bias for this dataset.

Feature Significance (Log Model):

Cement and Age are the most dominant features.
Water has a strong negative impact.
Superplasticizer becomes significant after transformation.
Both aggregates show weak influence.

Key Insights:

The log(x+1) transformation improves both model accuracy and generalization.
Proper preprocessing helps linear models perform better on real-world data.
The final model is interpretable and performs robustly on unseen samples.

Requirements:

Python 3.9 or higher
pandas
numpy
scikit-learn
matplotlib
seaborn


